"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[62201],{984582:e=>{e.exports="# T-SQL Dialect — Cross-Cutting Quirks & Behavioral Drift\n\nApplies to both `MSSQL` (SQL Server, Azure SQL Database, Azure SQL) and `SYNAPSE` (Azure Synapse Analytics). All patterns in this file apply to both dialects unless a section is explicitly marked `MSSQL only` or `SYNAPSE only`.\n\nLoaded eagerly before any pipeline step. Covers patterns that span all stages. Detailed date/time function mappings are in `references/tsql/10-functions-date.md` — fetch that during conversion when needed.\n\n## Cross-cutting syntax quirks\n\n### GO batch separator\n\nT-SQL uses `GO` as a batch separator (not a SQL keyword). Remove it before parsing:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nCREATE TABLE t (id INT);\nGO\nINSERT INTO t VALUES (1);\nGO\n\n-- DBSQL: remove GO, treat as separate statements\nCREATE TABLE t (id INT);\nINSERT INTO t VALUES (1);\n```\n\n### @@ global variables\n\n| T-SQL `@@` variable | DBSQL equivalent                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `@@ROWCOUNT`        | `ROW_COUNT()` — call immediately after DML                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| `@@ERROR`           | `SQLSTATE` in DECLARE handler                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `@@IDENTITY`        | No equivalent — use generated identity column separately                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `@@TRANCOUNT`       | No equivalent — no nested transaction tracking                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `@@SERVERNAME`      | No equivalent — `@@SERVERNAME` returns the SQL Server instance name (e.g., `MYSERVER\\SQLEXPRESS`). `current_catalog()` returns the Unity Catalog name, which is a different concept. If the source only uses `@@SERVERNAME` for logging or environment detection, replace with `current_catalog()` with an inline comment noting the semantic difference. |\n| `@@VERSION`         | No direct equivalent                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `@@FETCH_STATUS`    | Eliminated when cursor is rewritten as set-based                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n\n### Four-part names → Unity Catalog three-part names\n\nT-SQL: `linked_server.database.schema.table`\nDBSQL: `catalog.schema.table`\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nSELECT * FROM prod_server.salesdb.dbo.orders\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales.orders\n```\n\nTwo-part names (`dbo.tablename`): strip `dbo.` and use schema explicitly if needed.\n\n### SET options\n\nThese T-SQL `SET` options must be removed — they are not applicable in DBSQL:\n\n| T-SQL SET                             | Action                                      |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `SET NOCOUNT ON/OFF`                  | Remove                                      |\n| `SET ANSI_NULLS ON/OFF`               | Remove — **but see warning below**          |\n| `SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON/OFF`        | Remove                                      |\n| `SET XACT_ABORT ON/OFF`               | Remove                                      |\n| `SET ARITHABORT ON/OFF`               | Remove                                      |\n| `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...` | Remove (DBSQL uses MVCC snapshot isolation) |\n\n**`SET ANSI_NULLS OFF` — silent behavior change**: When ANSI_NULLS is OFF, T-SQL treats `NULL = NULL` as `TRUE` (non-standard). DBSQL always uses standard ANSI null semantics where `NULL = NULL` is `UNKNOWN`. If the source runs with `ANSI_NULLS OFF`, any comparison like `WHERE col = NULL` or `WHERE col1 = col2` where both can be NULL will silently change behavior after migration. Scan for these patterns and rewrite as `WHERE col IS NULL` or `WHERE col1 IS NOT DISTINCT FROM col2`.\n\n### Bracket identifiers → backticks\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nSELECT [order id], [first name] FROM [dbo].[orders]\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT `order id`, `first name` FROM orders\n```\n\n### WITH (NOLOCK) → remove\n\nDBSQL MVCC is always non-blocking; remove all locking hints:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nSELECT * FROM orders WITH (NOLOCK)\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT * FROM orders\n```\n\n## Behavioral drift\n\nFunctions that exist in both T-SQL and DBSQL but behave differently. These are the highest-risk patterns: they pass syntax validation and may pass intent checks but silently produce wrong results.\n\n### ISNULL — name collision (CRITICAL)\n\n| T-SQL                                                       | DBSQL                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ISNULL(expr, default)` returns `default` if `expr` is NULL | `ISNULL(col)` returns `TRUE` or `FALSE` (boolean predicate) |\n\n**Always map T-SQL `ISNULL(expr, default)` → `COALESCE(expr, default)`. Never leave `ISNULL(expr, default)` in DBSQL output.**\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nSELECT ISNULL(amount, 0) FROM orders   -- Returns 0 when amount is NULL\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — returns boolean!)\nSELECT ISNULL(amount, 0) FROM orders\n\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT)\nSELECT COALESCE(amount, 0) FROM orders\n```\n\n**`ISNULL(numericCol, '')` — implicit type coercion edge case**\n\nT-SQL `ISNULL(col, '')` where `col` is a non-string type (INT, DECIMAL, DATETIME) silently coerces the empty-string `''` to the column's type — producing `0`, `0.00`, `1900-01-01 00:00:00` (DATETIME zero-value), etc. The NULL replacement is _not_ an empty string — it is the type's coerced zero-value. `COALESCE(col, '')` does **not** replicate this and will fail with a type error if `col` is numeric or datetime.\n\nWhen translating `ISNULL(numericCol, '')`, choose the default that matches the **intended** semantics:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (amount is DECIMAL — NULL becomes 0, not '')\nISNULL(amount, '')   -- actual behavior: returns 0.00 when amount IS NULL\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: preserve the numeric zero-value behavior (faithful to what T-SQL actually does)\nCOALESCE(amount, 0)\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: preserve the '' intent (returns empty string for NULL; changes output type to STRING)\nCOALESCE(CAST(amount AS STRING), '')\n\n-- Add a comment explaining which semantics the source code relied on\n```\n\n### DATEDIFF — two forms in DBSQL, argument reversal, AND semantic difference\n\nT-SQL: `DATEDIFF(unit, start, end)` — counts **boundary crossings**\n\nDBSQL has **two forms**:\n\n- `DATEDIFF(endDate, startDate)` — 2-arg, **days only**, result is an `INTEGER`\n- `DATEDIFF(unit, start, end)` — 3-arg, any unit, synonym for `TIMESTAMPDIFF` (available DBR 10.4+); result is a `BIGINT`\n\nThe 2-arg form has **reversed arguments** relative to T-SQL. The 3-arg form has the **same argument order** as T-SQL but different semantics: DBSQL counts elapsed whole units, T-SQL counts boundary crossings.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (days)\nDATEDIFF(day, order_date, ship_date)\n\n-- DBSQL (days) — arguments reversed\nDATEDIFF(ship_date, order_date)\n\n-- T-SQL (months — boundary crossing)\nDATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: elapsed months (same arg order as T-SQL, but semantics differ for partial months)\nDATEDIFF(MONTH, d1, d2)     -- or equivalently: TIMESTAMPDIFF(MONTH, d1, d2)\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: boundary-crossing equivalent (matches T-SQL semantics exactly)\n(YEAR(d2)*12+MONTH(d2)) - (YEAR(d1)*12+MONTH(d1))\n```\n\n**When to use which**: If the source code relied on T-SQL boundary-crossing behavior (e.g., Dec 31 → Jan 1 = 1 month boundary), use the arithmetic form. If elapsed-unit semantics are acceptable, use `DATEDIFF(unit, start, end)`.\n\nFor year/month/quarter boundary-crossing equivalents, see `references/tsql/10-functions-date.md`.\n\n### CONCAT — NULL handling divergence\n\n| T-SQL                                                                        | DBSQL                                                                 |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CONCAT(a, b)` silently converts NULL arguments to `''` — never returns NULL | `CONCAT(a, b)` propagates NULL — returns NULL if any argument is NULL |\n\n**Check every T-SQL `CONCAT()` call where NULL inputs were expected to produce `''`.** If the source relies on NULL-swallowing, replace with `CONCAT_WS('', ...)` which skips NULLs in both systems.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: CONCAT silently swallows NULLs\nSELECT CONCAT('hello', NULL, 'world')  -- Returns 'helloworld'\n\n-- DBSQL: CONCAT propagates NULL\nSELECT CONCAT('hello', NULL, 'world')  -- Returns NULL\n\n-- DBSQL: use CONCAT_WS to skip NULLs (matches T-SQL CONCAT behavior)\nSELECT CONCAT_WS('', 'hello', NULL, 'world')  -- Returns 'helloworld'\n```\n\nNote: T-SQL string concatenation with `+` propagates NULL (same as DBSQL `CONCAT`). Only the multi-arg `CONCAT()` function differs.\n\n### Division operator — do not substitute try_divide\n\n`try_divide(a, b)` returns `NULL` on divide-by-zero whereas T-SQL `/` raises an error. Translate T-SQL division operators faithfully as `/`. Only use `try_divide` if the source code explicitly handles divide-by-zero (e.g. wraps the expression in a `CASE WHEN denominator = 0`).\n\n## Safe functions — no translation needed\n\n### CHARINDEX\n\n`CHARINDEX` is a **native DBSQL function** with identical syntax and semantics to T-SQL — leave it unchanged. Do not substitute with `INSTR`: `INSTR` has **reversed arguments** (`INSTR(string, substring)` vs `CHARINDEX(substring, string)`) and produces wrong results without argument swapping.\n\n### Case sensitivity — user-visible behavioral change (flag explicitly)\n\nT-SQL collations are often CI_AS (case-insensitive). DBSQL is case-sensitive by default.\n\n**This is a silent correctness risk**: if the source relies on case-insensitive string comparisons (WHERE clauses, JOIN conditions, string equality), those comparisons will change behavior after migration without any syntax error. Always call this out to the user in the output.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: WHERE name = 'John' matches 'JOHN', 'john', 'John' (CI_AS collation)\n-- DBSQL: WHERE name = 'John' only matches 'John' exactly\n\n-- DBSQL: to match case-insensitively:\nWHERE name COLLATE UTF8_LCASE = 'john'\n-- or at table level: define column with COLLATE UTF8_LCASE\n```\n\nWhen flagging this to the user, identify which specific comparisons are affected and suggest one of:\n\n1. Add `COLLATE UTF8_LCASE` to the affected comparison expressions\n2. Define the column with `COLLATE UTF8_LCASE` in the DDL (applies schema-wide)\n3. Normalize case on insert (`LOWER(col)`) if the data is user-controlled\n\n> For full DBSQL collation syntax and session-level collation settings, load `skills/writing-sql/collation.md`.\n\n### NULL ordering — ORDER BY behavior differs in both directions\n\nT-SQL treats NULLs as the **lowest** value: they appear **first** on `ASC`, **last** on `DESC`.\nDBSQL defaults: **NULLS LAST** on `ASC`, **NULLS FIRST** on `DESC`.\n\nThis means drift is always present in both directions when the source has an unqualified `ORDER BY`:\n\n| Direction           | T-SQL           | DBSQL default   | Fix                              |\n| ------------------- | --------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `ORDER BY col ASC`  | NULLs **first** | NULLs **last**  | Add `NULLS FIRST` to match T-SQL |\n| `ORDER BY col DESC` | NULLs **last**  | NULLs **first** | Add `NULLS LAST` to match T-SQL  |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nSELECT * FROM t ORDER BY salary DESC;     -- NULLs appear last\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — NULLs appear first by default on DESC)\nSELECT * FROM t ORDER BY salary DESC;\n\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT — matches T-SQL semantics)\nSELECT * FROM t ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS LAST;\n```\n\n**Scope**: only apply this fix where NULL position affects visible results — top-level `ORDER BY` and `OVER (... ORDER BY ...)` in window functions where the window value depends on row order (e.g. `ROW_NUMBER`, running totals). Ordering inside subqueries used for filtering or non-order-sensitive aggregations does not need `NULLS` qualifiers.\n\nApplies to: plain `ORDER BY`, `OVER (... ORDER BY ...)` in window functions, `WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)`.\n\n### TINYINT — unsigned vs signed (silent overflow)\n\nT-SQL `TINYINT` is unsigned (0–255). DBSQL `TINYINT` is signed (−128–127). Values 128–255 will silently overflow or error. Use `SMALLINT` as the safe migration target unless values are guaranteed ≤ 127. See `references/tsql/03-data-types.md`.\n\n### IDENTITY — disables parallel writes\n\n**Critical**: Declaring an identity column on a Delta table **disables parallel writes**. Multiple simultaneous writers will conflict. Consider `UUID()` for tables requiring concurrent writes.\n\nDDL mapping and alternatives: see `references/tsql/01-schema-ddl.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Construct Routing Table\n\nDuring the Plan step, scan the live source SQL for these patterns. Load the matching reference files via `readSkillFile` using the full paths shown. When adding a new reference file, update this table and `references/00-index.md` (human reference).\n\n| Pattern in source SQL                                                                                                                                                    | Reference file (full path for `readSkillFile`)                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cursors, `@@FETCH_STATUS` loops, `DECLARE CURSOR`                                                                                                                        | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/04-procedures-variables.md`   |\n| Temp tables (`#temp`, `##global`, table variables `DECLARE @t TABLE`)                                                                                                    | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/05-temp-tables-ctes.md`       |\n| `IDENTITY` columns, `CREATE TABLE`, `ALTER TABLE`, indexes, constraints                                                                                                  | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/01-schema-ddl.md`             |\n| Nested transactions, `SAVEPOINT`, `BEGIN TRAN`, `ROLLBACK`                                                                                                               | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/08-transactions-locking.md`   |\n| `NVARCHAR`, `VARCHAR(MAX)`, `CAST`, `CONVERT`, type declarations                                                                                                         | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/03-data-types.md`             |\n| `TOP`, `PIVOT`, `FOR XML PATH`, `UPDATE FROM JOIN`, `SELECT INTO`, system catalog queries (`sys.*`, `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.*`) — **not** `OBJECT_ID` alone (see note below) | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/02-select-dml.md`             |\n| `IF`/`ELSE`, `WHILE`, `BREAK`, `CONTINUE`, `GOTO`, `EXEC`, dynamic SQL                                                                                                   | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/09-scripting-control-flow.md` |\n| Stored procedures, UDFs, `DECLARE @var`, `SET @var`, `OUTPUT` parameters                                                                                                 | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/04-procedures-variables.md`   |\n| Date/time functions (`GETDATE`, `DATEADD`, `DATEDIFF`, `FORMAT`), CONVERT style codes                                                                                    | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/10-functions-date.md`         |\n| Query hints, `WITH (NOLOCK)`, `OPTION (RECOMPILE)`, statistics operations                                                                                                | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/07-performance-tuning.md`     |\n| SQL Agent jobs, multi-step batch pipelines                                                                                                                               | `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/06-etl-pipelines.md`          |\n| `GO`, `@@variables`, `[bracket identifiers]`, `SET NOCOUNT ON`, `ISNULL`, `CONCAT`, `DATEDIFF`                                                                           | Covered in this file — no additional load needed                                |\n\n**`OBJECT_ID` exception**: `IF OBJECT_ID('dbo.tablename') IS NOT NULL DROP TABLE dbo.tablename` translates directly to `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS tablename` — no reference file needed. Only load `02-select-dml.md` if `OBJECT_ID` appears in a `sys.columns` query or other system catalog lookup that requires a full mapping.\n\n**Dynamic SQL — only the dynamic portion is untranslatable**: If a procedure builds a SQL string in a variable (`@SQL`, `@WHERE`, or similar) and executes it via `EXEC(@variable)` or `sp_executesql @SQL`, that specific dynamic-SQL block cannot be mechanically translated. **Translate the rest of the procedure normally** — static DML, control flow, temp table operations, error handling, and CURSOR logic are all translatable and must not be omitted.\n\nMark only the untranslatable dynamic-SQL block with a TODO:\n\n```sql\n-- TODO: procedure builds and executes dynamic SQL — manual rewrite required\n```\n\nDo not collapse the entire procedure into a single TODO block. A procedure with one dynamic-SQL statement and ten other static statements should produce a translated procedure with nine translated statements and one TODO comment.\n\n**Exception — purely dynamic procedures**: When the _entire_ procedure body consists of string-building logic that feeds a single `EXEC`/`sp_executesql` (no extractable static DML), then there is no translatable code to produce and a procedure-level TODO is appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## Large-file Chunking\n\nWhen the source exceeds 300 lines, run `grepAsset` with this pattern to find batch boundaries:\n\n```\n^\\s*GO\\s*$\n```\n\nIf at least one `GO` line is found, process each GO-separated section independently. If no `GO` lines are found, process as a single unit.\n\n---\n\n## TODO Reference\n\n### Constructs that cannot be translated (valid TODO)\n\nThese T-SQL patterns have no Databricks SQL equivalent. Mark them with `-- TODO: manual review required — <reason>` and move on.\n\n| T-SQL construct                                                      | Reason                                                                   |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `xp_cmdshell`                                                        | OS shell access — no DBSQL equivalent                                    |\n| `sp_executesql` / `EXEC(@variable)` with dynamic parameter injection | Dynamic SQL built from string concatenation — see dynamic SQL note above |\n| Linked server queries (`linked_server.db.schema.table`)              | Requires external data source configuration outside the migration scope  |\n| `OPENROWSET`, `OPENQUERY`                                            | Ad-hoc remote queries — no direct DBSQL equivalent                       |\n| `CLR` assemblies                                                     | .NET integration — not supported in DBSQL                                |\n\n### Constructs that ARE supported (not valid TODO reasons)\n\nThese T-SQL patterns have direct Databricks SQL equivalents. Use the reference files — do not use TODO as a substitute for looking up the translation.\n\n| T-SQL construct                                         | DBSQL equivalent                                     | Reference file                 |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| Cursors (`DECLARE CURSOR`, `FETCH`, `@@FETCH_STATUS`)   | `DECLARE`/`FETCH` in SQL warehouse context           | `04-procedures-variables.md`   |\n| Temp tables (`#temp`, `##global`, `DECLARE @t TABLE`)   | `CREATE TEMP TABLE` or CTE                           | `05-temp-tables-ctes.md`       |\n| Error handling (`BEGIN TRY / BEGIN CATCH`, `RAISERROR`) | `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION`              | `04-procedures-variables.md`   |\n| Complex control flow (`IF`/`ELSE`, `WHILE`, `GOTO`)     | Direct equivalents in DBSQL scripting                | `09-scripting-control-flow.md` |\n| `MERGE`                                                 | `MERGE` (supported in DBSQL with syntax differences) | `08-transactions-locking.md`   |\n\n---\n\n## Validation Checklist\n\nAction table moved to `references/tsql/00-validation-patterns.md` — load that file via `readSkillFile` during the Validate step. It contains both the combined `grepAsset` regex and the pattern → action rows.\n"}}]);
